[#:seon.task{:name "entrance-stages-select-no-tests"
             :intent "An edit to the MCP entrance's own stages selects no test: `my.program/tests-reaching` answers 0 gating tests for `seon.mcp/jvm-run`, `seon.mcp/sci-run` and `seon.mcp/observed` (1 for eval-clj, 6 for call; default, 2026-09-28). Selection is one caller deep, the entrance tests reach `seon.mcp/call` through a test-private helper, and call names eval-clj as data in its tool case, so a dogfooded change to the stages that cost the run proves nothing on its branch. Done when an edit to each seon.mcp stage selects the mcp-entrance tests."
             :must-read #{seon.mcp/call seon.fn/gate-set}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/mcp-dogfood--probe.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "entrance-profile-counts-the-requested-evaluation"
             :intent "The health report ranked `seon.mcp/jvm-run` at mean 1.06 s, max 14.0 s over 41 calls, but the armed profile of the entrance includes the evaluation the agent asked for (test runs, probes) and each restart's cold hosting (first call after a restart 3.6 s: host! 2.6 s, acquire-context! 1.45 s; a new agent's first call 180-330 ms). The warm entrance around a trivial form is 57-90 ms JVM and 88-100 ms SCI. The batch already measures its stages (:seon.batch/timings read, evaluate, settle); done when the report ranks the entrance by its own stages, not by the work requested through it."
             :must-read #{seon.mcp/call seon.batch/evaluate-batch}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/mcp-dogfood--probe.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "jvm-mode-replies-omit-time-calls-and-location",
            :intent
            "MCP eval_clj in JVM mode answers errors with no :ms, no :calls and no source location: (throw (ex-info \"jvm boom\" {})) and (Integer/parseInt \"nope\") answered only :error and :result, while the same forms in SCI mode carry :ms and `:seon.sci.eval/stacktrace`. Correct: both modes answer the same reply keys (time, armed calls, the form's location). Proof: a JVM-mode failing form's reply carries `:seon.repl/ms`.",
            :must-read #{seon.mcp/call}}
 #:seon.task{:name "jvm-mode-defs-live-forever-with-no-owner"
             :intent "Lanes' MCP `mode jvm` evaluations intern vars that live as long as the JVM and nothing reports or retires them: `user/ev` and `user/cap` hold 440,605 read-evidence maps (448 MB, 34% of the 1.16-1.31 GB live set after full GC), `user/db` pins an old database, `tmp.codec-print/*` (heap-live lane MAT, default, 2026-10-08). Options: JVM mode evaluates in a per-agent namespace retired with the agent (`my.agent/retire-branch!`), and/or runtime_status reports large interned vars by namespace. Done when a def made through `seon.mcp/jvm-run` by an agent is gone after that agent retires."
             :must-read #{seon.mcp/jvm-run my.agent/retire-branch!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "eval-clj-advertises-no-timeout"
             :intent "`seon.mcp/eval-arguments` and `seon.mcp/tool-table` now declare the timeout and `seon.mcp-entrance-test/eval-clj-advertises-the-timeout-the-bridge-honours` passes. Complete the installed path: a freshly connected MCP client must receive that argument schema and a bounded `my.test/check-platform` request must return its result through the bridge. Preserve an end-to-end proof; the tool-table unit test alone does not establish that the connected client or bridge uses the new bound."
             :must-read #{seon.mcp/tool-table seon.mcp/eval-arguments my.test/check-platform}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fresh-agents-first-eval-request-acquires-its-branch-cheaply"
             :intent "A fresh agent's first `mcp__seon__eval_clj` request spends 165-178 ms (restart audit) and 148.6 ms (re-measured on default 2026-10-12, agent `findings`, `(+ 1 2)`: `:custody` 148.6 of 257 ms) in the reply's `:custody` stage, the time from the entrance to the return of `seon.mcp/execution`, which for an unknown agent id runs `my.agent/create-branch!` (a `datahike.api/branch!` plus the agent's rows) and the agent's start. A branch is a pointer to shared immutable indexes, so it should cost one `d/branch!` and one recording transaction (single-digit ms, `seon.profile/measure`). Find the armed functions under `seon.mcp/execution` that own the 148 ms (profile cells before/after), and change that owner; phase E3 (`seon.cluster.agent/a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork`) covers the SCI fork part only. Done when the deftest the implementer writes first, `seon.mcp-test/a-fresh-agents-first-eval-request-acquires-its-branch-cheaply`, asserts through `seon.profile/measure` that the `:custody` stage of a first request is under the measured bound on the named armed functions."
             :must-read #{seon.mcp/execution my.agent/create-branch! seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/restart-audit/cache-hits.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/restart-audit/request-cost.md"}}
]
